<h1>Understanding Renewals</h1>

<p>
    Renewals are the central hub for managing everything related to policy renewals. Think of a renewal as a workspace that brings together all the pieces you need to successfully renew a client's policy—tasks, quotes from different carriers, proposals, and important documents.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot of the Renewals board/calendar view showing multiple renewals with different statuses (In Progress, Renewed, etc.) and expiration dates. Should show the visual layout of how renewals are organized. -->

<h2>What is a Renewal?</h2>

<p>
    A renewal represents a policy that's coming up for renewal. When you create a renewal, Quickfire automatically sets up a workspace where you can:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Track tasks</strong> – Create a checklist of everything that needs to happen before the policy expires</li>
    <li><strong>Manage submissions</strong> – Keep track of quotes you're getting from different carriers and wholesalers</li>
    <li><strong>Store documents</strong> – Attach loss runs, applications, quotes, and other important files</li>
    <li><strong>Monitor progress</strong> – See at a glance where each renewal stands in the process</li>
</ul>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot of a renewal detail page showing the main sections: header with client info, task board, submissions grid, and attachments. Annotate the key areas with callouts. -->

<h2>How Renewals Are Created</h2>

<p>
    Renewals can be created automatically or manually:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>From a policy</strong> – When you have an existing policy, you can create a renewal directly from it. The system will copy important information like the policy number, premium, carrier, and expiration date.</li>
    <li><strong>Manually</strong> – You can also create a renewal from scratch if you're working with a new client or a policy that isn't in the system yet.</li>
</ul>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot showing the "Create Renewal from Policy" button or menu option, with an arrow pointing to where this action is located. -->

<h2>Renewal Status</h2>

<p>
    Each renewal has a status that tells you where it is in the renewal process. Common statuses include:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>In Progress</strong> – The renewal is actively being worked on</li>
    <li><strong>Renewed</strong> – The policy has been successfully renewed</li>
    <li><strong>Pending</strong> – Waiting for something (like a quote or client decision)</li>
    <li><strong>Unneeded</strong> – The client doesn't need to renew this policy</li>
    <li><strong>Defected</strong> – The client moved to a different agency</li>
    <li><strong>Ghosted</strong> – The client stopped responding</li>
    <li><strong>Unplaceable</strong> – Unable to find coverage</li>
</ul>

<p>
    The status updates automatically in some cases—for example, when a submission is marked as "Bound," the renewal status will change to "Renewed." You can also manually update the status at any time.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot of the renewal status dropdown menu showing all available status options. Highlight the current status and show how to change it. -->

<h2>Key Information on a Renewal</h2>

<p>
    When you open a renewal, you'll see several important pieces of information:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Client and Policy Details</strong> – Who the renewal is for and which policy is expiring</li>
    <li><strong>Expiration Date</strong> – When the current policy expires (this is your deadline!)</li>
    <li><strong>Expiring Premium</strong> – What the client is currently paying</li>
    <li><strong>Carrier and Wholesaler</strong> – The current insurance company and any broker involved</li>
    <li><strong>Assigned To</strong> – Which team member is responsible for this renewal</li>
    <li><strong>Bill Type</strong> – How the client pays (Direct Bill, Agency Bill, etc.)</li>
</ul>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Close-up screenshot of the renewal header/info section with labels pointing to each field mentioned above. -->

<h2>The Renewal Workflow</h2>

<p>
    Here's a typical flow for working with a renewal:
</p>

<ol>
    <li><strong>Create the renewal</strong> – Set it up when you know a policy is coming up for renewal</li>
    <li><strong>Apply a task group</strong> – Add a pre-made checklist of tasks (or create tasks manually)</li>
    <li><strong>Create submissions</strong> – Add quotes you're getting from carriers and wholesalers</li>
    <li><strong>Track progress</strong> – Update task statuses and submission statuses as you work</li>
    <li><strong>Send proposals</strong> – Once you have quotes, create and send proposals to the client</li>
    <li><strong>Bind coverage</strong> – When the client accepts, mark the submission as "Bound"</li>
    <li><strong>Complete tasks</strong> - Finish any remaining tasks like updating your AMS or sending documents</li>
</ol>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Flowchart or visual diagram showing the renewal workflow from creation to completion, with icons for each step. -->

<h2>Tips for Success</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Keep statuses updated</strong> – Accurate statuses help your team and show up correctly on dashboards and reports</li>
    <li><strong>Use task groups</strong> – Save time by using pre-made task groups instead of creating tasks one by one</li>
    <li><strong>Attach documents early</strong> – Upload loss runs and applications as soon as you have them so they're easy to find</li>
    <li><strong>Update regularly</strong> – Even small updates help keep everyone on the same page</li>
    <li><strong>Check the activity log</strong> – See a complete history of who did what and when</li>
</ul>

